  "body": "Custom elements created by the parser don't have the same level of coordination that script end tags do: They're not parser blocking script, and they don't take care of updating the insertion point. For example, if I have\n\n``` html\n<script>customElements.define('a-a', ...);</script>\n<a-a>\n```\n\nI believe the `</script>` runs the script and resets the insertion point to the initial, undefined, value. Then document.write in a-a's constructor should result in open?\n\nI'll admit I don't 100% understand this, so I probably got some of this wrong, but I wonder if it is better to disallow document.write during custom element construction by incrementing the ignore destructive writes counter? It looks particularly tricky to use given the behavior hooks it has are tuned to script running, not custom element running.\n",
